  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   We propose a reinforcement learning (RL) agent that interactively improves sequence candidates using the AC1 score as a reward signal. Unlike genetic algorithms, this method balances exploration and exploitation through neural network guidance. The core idea is to:\n   - Represent sequences as continuous vectors in high-dimensional space\n   - Use a policy gradient algorithm to learn effective mutation operators\n   - Prioritize promising regions of the search space\n   - Maintain diversity through entropy bonuses\n\n   The key differentiator is the use of learned representations to identify beneficial mutations, guided by the RL framework. This approach escapes local optima by adaptively refining search directions based on historical scores.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   Variables: \n   - Sequence: real-valued vector s \u2208 \u211d\u1d38 (L=352) with s\u1d62 \u2208 [0, 1000]\n   - Policy parameterized by neural network \u03b8: \u211d\u1d38 \u2192 \u211d\u1d38 (logits)\n   - Learning objective: maximize expected AC1 score\n   - Reward function: \n        R(s) = 1 - AC1(s)   (maximize)\n   - Action: proportional perturbation of each component\n        s' = (s + \u03b5\u2299a)\u2299clamp(\u03bb)   where a_i = \u03c3(\u03b8(s))\n        \u03b5 ~ \u03f5-net: uniform(-\u03b5, \u03b5) with \u03b5=min(\u03a3/sqrt(L), 5)\n        clamp(\u03bb): floor at min(0, \u03bb*median_active), cap at \u03bb*max(1000, \u03a3)\n\n   Constraints handled through:\n   - Implicit enforcement via reparameterization\n   - Search space clipping\n   - Diversion to manual mutation on failure\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   a. Load parent sequence if available (if height_sequence_1 exists)\n   b. Initialize:\n        \u03b8 = Xavier-initialized neural network (1 hidden layer: 512 units)\n        learner = Adam(learning_rate=0.001, clip_value=0.5)\n        replay_buffer = deque(maxlen=1000)\n        best_candidate = None\n        best_score = \u221e\n        exploration_rate = 0.9 (exponentially decayed)\n\n   c. Core loop:\n        Generate 5^4=1250 training samples from current population\n        Normalize scores to per-rendezvous style\n        For each mini-batch of 256 samples:\n            Forward pass\n            Sample actions\n            Compute policy gradients\n            Update parameters via temporal difference target\n            Gradually decay exploration\n\n        For each candidate in population:\n            Generate crossover/mutation based on policy output\n            Clip/truncate/normalize\n            Evaluate AC1 score\n\n        Update best_candidate with top-scoring candidate (highest reward)\n\n   d. Structured update operators:\n      - Crossover: blend highest-scoring sequences\n      - Mutation: guided by neural network activations\n      - Mutation net Score: (score * \u2207score / |\u2207score|)\n\n   e. Safety checks:\n      - Nan/inf filters\n      - Resource monitoring\n      - Temperature-distilled exploration\n      - Progressive evaluation\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n   a. Temperature parameter \u03c4: 0.3 (controls policy sharpness)\n   b. Mutation police net: [0.1, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0] (per-vector parameter)\n   c. Strategic initialization: prioritize neighborhoods of parent\n   d. Adaptive learning rate schedule\n   e. Multi-timescale exploration\n\n5. Computation Budget and Robustness:\n   - Dominant cost: 100 mini-batches * 256 samples = 25,600 evaluations\n   - Time estimate: 100ms/evaluation * 25,600 \u2248 2,560 seconds (allow ~2x)\n   - Multi-threaded evaluation using np.convolve's FFT-vectorization\n   - Progressive heuristic: if >5 generations without improvement \u22b2 respawn population\n   - Fallback: restart with 50% mutated parent\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   - Pre-evaluate 3x the generation population\n   - Caching for duplicate candidates\n   - Final scoring via exact_sequence_evaluator\n   - Reward shaping:\n        reward1 = AC1(candidate)   # proactive termination\n        reward2 = gradient_signedness(\u2207score)  # quality propellant\n        final_reward = reward2 + (100 * reward1_min) / (attempt_number+1)\n   - Return format: \n        [round_val(min(x, 1000)) for x in best_candidate]\n        Ensure sum+0.001 before normalization\n\n   Post-cached history_best: [lastFoundGoodEvalResult]",
